## Socket.io vs Raw WebSocket

## Raw WebSocket

```javascript
// Browser
const ws = new WebSocket('wss://api.example.com');
ws.onopen = () => ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'chat', text: 'Hello' }));
ws.onmessage = (e) => console.log(JSON.parse(e.data));
ws.onclose = () => { /* reconnect manually */ };

// You must implement:
// - Reconnection logic
// - Event routing (inspect message.type)
// - Room/broadcast logic (server-side)
// - Heartbeat/ping-pong
```

## Socket.io Features

```javascript
// Client
import { io } from 'socket.io-client';
const socket = io('https://api.example.com');

socket.on('chat message', (data) => updateUI(data));
socket.emit('join room', { room: 'general' });
socket.emit('chat message', { text: 'Hello' });

// Auto-reconnects, handles fallbacks, named events built-in
```

```javascript
// Server (Node.js)
import { Server } from 'socket.io';
const io = new Server(httpServer);

io.on('connection', (socket) => {
  socket.on('join room', ({ room }) => socket.join(room));
  socket.on('chat message', (data) => {
    io.to(data.room).emit('chat message', data); // broadcast to room
  });
});
```

## Feature Comparison

| Feature | Raw WebSocket | Socket.io |
|---|---|---|
| Auto-reconnect | ❌ Manual | ✅ Built-in |
| Long-polling fallback | ❌ | ✅ |
| Named events | ❌ | ✅ |
| Rooms/namespaces | ❌ | ✅ |
| Broadcasting | ❌ (manual) | ✅ |
| Bundle size | 0 kb (native) | ~45 kb |
| Server dependency | Any WS lib | Socket.io server |

## When to Use Raw WebSocket

- Mobile/embedded clients where bundle size matters
- When the server is not Node.js
- When Socket.io features aren't needed (simple 1:1 connection)

